18. Research Program
• Cross-model reconstruction benchmark: give compressed Seals/artifacts to independent models and measure invariant recovery.
• Ablation benchmark: remove Router, Metamap, Afterglow, Reality, or O-IOE and measure capability loss.
• Representation tournament: solve the same task through multiple renderings and compare fidelity/cost/novelty.
• Operator chemistry matrix: estimate noncommutative order effects across problem classes.
• Portal breeder: promote repeated multi-hop desire-lines into tested direct transforms.
• Novelty benchmark: distinguish lexical novelty from new addressability/capability.
• Recursive calibration benchmark: test whether learned correction gains outperform fixed gains.
19. Relationship to Existing Research
Pantheon overlaps with representation learning, concept formation, program synthesis, meta-learning, ontology engineering, novelty search, open-ended evolution, automated discovery, control theory, and agent architectures. Novelty Search showed that objective-only search can be deceptive; modern open-ended work explicitly studies expansive/transformational search spaces and foundation-model-guided interestingness. Recent self-improving-agent work also validates code changes empirically rather than relying on proof alone. Pantheon should therefore claim a particular integration and executable vocabulary, not ownership of those underlying fields.

